     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. I can't verify the protocol or the pharmacist review, and I don't know of any protocol that supports metformin at 5,100 mg/day. That's twice the maximum labeled dose, and a 1,700 mg single dose is also well above the usual per-dose limit. The main risk is metformin-associated lactic acidosis, and monitoring can detect that harm but can't prevent it.\n\nHere are some ways to move forward:\n- **Enter a dose within labeled limits.** For example, 850 mg PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day). I can enter that now.\n- **Go through pharmacy.** If a real high-dose protocol exists, the pharmacist can verify and enter or co-sign the order, with the protocol name or reference attached. That way the override is documented and checked by the system, not just by me.\n- **Correct the dose if it was a typo.** Give me the intended dose and I'll enter it.\n\nWhich would you like?"
      }
     ],
